------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 6 — Initial Conditions
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Galaxy components (disk, halo) are generated using analytic distribution
functions. Each particle stores:

- Position (x, y, z)
- Velocity (vx, vy, vz)
- Mass
- Component identifier

Initial conditions are constructed independently for MW and M31, then shifted
and boosted to match observational constraints on relative separation and
velocity.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 7 — Barnes–Hut Tree Implementation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section documents:
- Octree node structure
- Recursive subdivision logic
- Center-of-mass accumulation
- Opening-angle acceptance criterion

Special attention is paid to:
- Avoiding self-force contributions
- Consistent softening treatment
- Numerical stability during close encounters


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 8 — Time Integration
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The leapfrog integrator is implemented in a strictly symplectic
kick–drift–kick form.

If adaptive timestep control is enabled:
- The timestep is computed from the maximum acceleration magnitude
- A global timestep cap is enforced
- The criterion is fully vectorized


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 9 — Diagnostics
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section introduces fully vectorized diagnostics:

- Kinetic energy:
      K = 1/2 ∑ m_i v_i²

- Potential energy (direct, for small N):
      U = -1/2 ∑_{i≠j} G m_i m_j / r_ij

- Angular momentum:
      L = ∑ m_i (r_i × v_i)

These diagnostics are used to assess numerical stability and conservation
properties.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 10 — Barnes–Hut Validation Against Direct Gravity
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For small particle subsets:
- Accelerations are computed using both Barnes–Hut and direct N² gravity
- Relative L2 error is measured
- The dependence on opening angle and softening length is examined

This provides quantitative confidence in the approximation accuracy.


